--TEST--
An anonymous function may not declare an `inout $x` parameter, in either body form — `rule:types/implicit-capture`, § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A by-reference parameter is a contract between a *call site* and a
// declaration: the site stages the cell, hands over its address and copies
// back afterwards. An anonymous function's type is `callable` and nothing else (`rule:types/callable-is-the-only-function-type`
// ), so it carries no parameter list for a site to read and no site can
// know to stage anything — and § 2's by-value capture lets the function outlive
// every frame in scope where it was written, so naming a cell would not make
// one outlast it. That is the same reason § 2 has no `use (&$y)`.
callable $bump = fn (inout int $n): int => $n + 1;

// The block body reaches the same rule, and each parameter is its own report.
callable $pair = fn (inout int $a, inout int $b): int => {
    return $a + $b;
};

// Sharing a mutable cell is § 2's own answer: an object, captured by value.
final class Counter {
    public int $value = 0;
}
Counter $c = new Counter();
callable $step = fn (): int => $c->value + 1;
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0493]: an anonymous function cannot take `$n` as `inout`
  --> case.nvs:9:32
%A
error[E0493]: an anonymous function cannot take `$a` as `inout`
   --> case.nvs:12:32
%A
error[E0493]: an anonymous function cannot take `$b` as `inout`
   --> case.nvs:12:46
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
